Linked Lists are the foundation of many Data Structures. 

Stack and Queue: Implements a Stack or Queue using either an Array
or Linked List 
Array Back: Worries about size
List Back: Does not have to worry about size

Nodes have values, but the structure is actually maintained by pointers 
and references to other nodes

Ex: node(100). node(200), and node (300) are all apart of a linked list
100 has to point to 200, and 200 has to point to 300
To search through the list, we need a HEAD pointer to point to 100
This is called a single Link List, because we can only go one way through
the Link List. 

The Advantage of inserting with a Linked List as opposed to an Array, is
that LL only needs to point to the insert, and the insert point to the
next node. With an array, you would need to shift all array values, due
to it being a contiguous data structure within Random Access Memory. 
Linked List are Sequential Access since they are not contiguous

You have to maintain the HEAD Pointer, or else you'll lose the List

The TAIL Pointer, will point to the end of the list
you can use the TAIL Pointer to insert a Node at the end of a List
(Make sure to always update TAIL after each append)

Append(insert)- WC: O(n) BC: O(1)
Traversal(search)- WC: O(n)
Deletion- WC: O(n) BC: O(1) 


